# Local developer infrastructure: self-service, scoped, fail-closed

Read [worktree-owned dev stacks](../../docs/runbooks/dev-worktree-stacks.md) when a Change needs a live application runtime. For ordinary implementation, start/status/stop **your own canonical worktree** through its repository scripts once T05 has passed the documented live acceptance. Discover assigned slot, URLs, Pod names and backing resources through the script's status/runbook only if needed; do not require an owner to hand-select stacks.

**Important rollout limitation (inspected `5d8be46c`):** root `pnpm dev` and `pnpm dev:status`/`pnpm dev:stop` now exist and a two-slot trial partially passed. However, new-worktree unattended provisioning, the full source watcher, actual runtime freshness, and safe PID/start-time lease lifecycle are not proven. Use these scripts only when the DXK09 T05 gate is explicitly marked proven in the [runbook](../../docs/runbooks/dev-worktree-stacks.md); otherwise return the specific missing platform capability to DXK09. Do not improvise shared lab provisioning or silently accept a static snapshot.

Local infrastructure problems are part of authorized work **within the selected target**, not permission for a global reset. Use explicit `kind-offscreen-dev` context for worktree apps, and preserve `kind-offscreen-lab` release acceptance, Compose, other slots, database/S3/Temporal tenants and credentials. Use focused test dependencies and package builds as needed. Never assume ambient kubectl context selects the correct purpose.

Stop for foreign ownership/UID/port/process conflicts, unverified stale leases, unsafe resource admission, paid providers, credentials exposure, destructive data retirement, remote/non-disposable data mutation or an architectural contradiction. An abandoned Cursor chat is not proof a tenant can be deleted. No global `tilt down`, blanket `kind delete`, reset or PID-only kill. 
